From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 00:15:28 +0200
Subject: ASoC: qdsp6: q6voice: voice call session management

A voice call on this family never passes through the application
processor: the modem exchanges speech with the ADSP internally and the
ADSP drives the codec over the AFE ports. The AP's only job is to set the
session up, over three APR services -- MVM, CVS and CVP.

The part that is easy to get wrong, and which reports success while
wrong: the modem creates its own MVM and CVS during init, and a PASSIVE
control session attaches to those rather than making new ones. Everything
the modem has already done then answers ADSP_EALREADY, which is a
statement of fact, not a failure. Creating a session while the modem has
none instead returns a freshly allocated handle and status 0 from every
command in the sequence, and carries no call audio at all -- so status
cannot be taken as evidence that the path is live.

Teardown destroys only the vocproc for the same reason. Destroying a
joined MVM or CVS does not merely end the call: the modem builds them
once, so every later call then finds nothing to attach to and is silent
until the modem restarts.

TOPOLOGY_ID_NONE is deliberately not offered. It is for local call hold,
not a neutral passthrough -- selecting it for TX killed the downlink and
the SoC was reset by the watchdog seconds later. Neither of the two
topologies used here needs calibration data.

The ports are the caller's to choose and must already be running: the
ADSP renders into them but does not start them.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
